#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163340 :tutorial/content "For continuous random variables $X$ and $Y,$ the **conditional expectation of $X$ given $Y=y$** is defined as\n\n$$\n\\textrm E[X |Y = y] =\\int\\limits_{\\mathbb R} x \\, f_{X|Y}(x|y)\\;\\textrm dx,\n$$\n\nwhere $f_{X|Y}(x|y)$ is the conditional probability density function of $X$ given $Y.$ \n\nThis definition is similar to that of $\\textrm{E}[X].$ However, we now use the conditional PDF $f_{X|Y}(x|y)$ instead of the marginal PDF $f_X(x)$ in our integral.\n\nNote the following:\n\n- When we select a particular value of $y,$ the conditional expected value of $X$ given $Y=y$ returns a real number. However, if $y$ is not specified, i.e., $\\textrm{E}[X |Y = y]$ *depends* on $y,$ we have that $\\textrm{E}[X |Y = y]$ gives us a *function* of $y.$\n\n- If $X$ and $Y$ are independent, then $\\textrm E[X | Y = y] = \\textrm E[X]$ for all $y$.\n\n- We sometimes use the notation $\\mu_{X|y}$ to denote $E[X | Y = y].$\n\n- We have an analogous definition for $\\textrm E[Y |X = x]\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\textrm E[Y |X = x] =\\int\\limits_{\\mathbb R} y \\, f_{Y|X}(y|x)\\;\\textrm dy,\n$$\n\nwhere $f_{Y|X}(y|x)$ is the conditional probability density function of $Y$ given $X.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "9640f2b9-e8e6-51dd-8c81-c7f1a9f45362" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6428 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163348 :tutorial/content "Let's find the conditional expectation $\\textrm{E}\\big[X \\,|\\, Y=1\\big]$ given that the conditional probability density function $f_{X|Y}(x \\,|\\, 1)$ is given by\n![](images/8b/8b71da7aecae81ed4e8d9dc27f4730d3d0a03d73146b7b899003d3f8787694bc.svg) \n\nApplying the formula for the conditional expectation of $X$ given $Y=y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X\\,|\\,Y=1] & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }x\\,{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}x\\cdot 2(x-1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}2x(x-1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}2{x}^{2}-2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{3}-{x}^{2}]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =[\\frac{2(2)^{3}}{3}-(2)^{2}]-[\\frac{2(1)^{3}}{3}-(1)^{2}] \\\\  & =[\\frac{16}{3}-4]-[\\frac{2}{3}-1] \\\\  & =[\\frac{16}{3}-\\frac{12}{3}]-[\\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{3}{3}] \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}-(-\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "8c2314f8-7929-53f2-9261-e2cc145337ae" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6481 :tutorial/title "A Worked Example"}]}
